 "steps": [
  {
   "id": "s0",
   "title": "A block of ice",
   "text": "Ice is a **solid**: each molecule vibrates around a fixed place in a regular lattice. This is a very ordered arrangement with low entropy. We now add heat at a steady rate.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "q": 0
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "title": "Heating the solid",
   "text": "The heat raises the temperature: the molecules vibrate harder. The **T–Q graph** climbs.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "q": 0
     },
     "at": 0
    },
    {
     "tween": {
      "q": 0.995
     },
     "at": 0,
     "dur": 3800
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "title": "Melting: temperature stays at 0 °C",
   "text": "At the melting point the heat no longer raises the temperature. It goes into **breaking the bonds** between molecules. The graph is **flat**, yet the molecules are escaping their places.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "q": 1
     },
     "at": 0
    },
    {
     "tween": {
      "q": 1.995
     },
     "at": 0,
     "dur": 4800
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "title": "Entropy has risen",
   "text": "The ordered lattice has turned into a disordered liquid: many more arrangements are possible, so the **entropy is higher**, although the temperature did not change. Heat Q absorbed at constant T gives \\( \\Delta S = Q/T \\).",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "q": 2
     },
     "at": 0
    },
    {
     "tween": {
      "q": 2.4
     },
     "at": 0,
     "dur": 2500
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s4",
   "title": "Heating the water",
   "text": "With all the bonds broken the added heat again raises the temperature. Press play on **Heat added** to run the whole sequence.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "q": 2
     },
     "at": 0
    },
    {
     "tween": {
      "q": 2.995
     },
     "at": 0,
     "dur": 3200
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
